# Standard library: geometry
#
# Points, distances and angles in the plane, triangles, and congruence.
#
# One record per item. A record begins at column 0 with its kind, axiom,
# theorem or definition, and its name; `mundane` before the kind marks an
# item a proof takes for granted. A proof imports each item it cites,
# `import <header> stdlib/geometry/<name>`, where the header is the
# record's own before its name, as `mundane theorem` or `axiom`. Format is described in DATABASE.md.
#
# An item's statement is written in the theorem form of SYNTAX.md: let and
# assume lines with labels, then a `then` line.
#
# Every item carries at least one of:
#   metamath    a set.mm label or labels supply it
#   open        it is cited but unproved and unbridged
#
# Other fields: target, note, and for the one definition that
# introduces a symbol, symbol and defines.


mundane definition point
  then        A is a point ↔ A ∈ 𝔼²
  metamath    biid, since "A is a point" and A ∈ 𝔼² are both built as A ∈ ℂ
  target      biid
  note        A point is a member of the plane. The plane is ℂ in the kernel,
              which the page never says (GEOMETRY.md).

mundane definition angle
  symbol      ang
  defines     vx vy cc cc0 csn cdif cc cc0 csn cdif
              vy cv vx cv cdiv co clog cfv cim cfv cmpo
  metamath    set.mm writes this function inline in every theorem about it and
              never names it, so the corpus declares the constant
  note        The angle the corpus writes is unsigned: ∠PQR is the absolute
              value of ang applied to the two differences, which lands in
              0 to π and makes mun:stdlib/geometry/angle-symmetric true. ang itself is
              signed, in −π to π, as set.mm has it. GEOMETRY.md weighs the
              seven candidate geometries and takes this one.

              This is the only definition in the corpus that introduces a
              symbol; every other names a word for something set.mm already
              has. See GOALS.md decision 12. Its statement is `defines`, what
              the symbol stands for, and what follows from that is
              mun:stdlib/geometry/angle-real.

mundane theorem angle-real
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        ∠PQR ∈ ℝ. ∠PQR ≥ 0.
  metamath    gangbnd3, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gangbnd3
  note        ∠PQR is an absolute value, so it is real and at least 0; what
              makes that a proof is that ang of the two differences is a
              number, which gangrange gives by putting ∠PQR in [0, π].

mundane theorem signed-angle-size
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        |∡PQR| = ∠PQR
  metamath    eqid, since ∠PQR is built as the absolute value of ∡PQR
  target      eqid
  note        The angle is the signed angle's size, whichever way it turns.

mundane theorem signed-angle-real
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        ∡PQR ∈ ℝ. −π < ∡PQR. ∡PQR ≤ π.
  metamath    gsangcl, from angcld
  target      gsangcl

mundane theorem signed-angle-reverse
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  assume ∡PQR ≠ π                                                     (H6)
  then        ∡RQP = −∡PQR
  metamath    gsangrev, from logrec
  target      gsangrev
  note        Turned the other way, the signed angle changes sign; at π, a
              straight angle, it is π both ways.

theorem signed-angle-sum
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume Q ≠ R                                                        (H5)
  assume P ≠ R                                                        (H6)
  then        ∡RQP + ∡PRQ + ∡QPR ∈ {−π, π}
  metamath    gang180, from ang180
  target      gang180
  note        The signed angles of a triangle, each turned the same way
              around it, add to π or to −π as the triangle runs
              counterclockwise or clockwise. The three points need only be
              apart: on one line the angles are 0, 0 and π.

mundane theorem signed-angle-zero
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H3)
  then        ∡PQP = 0
  metamath    gsangid, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from log1
  target      gsangid
  note        A ray turned to itself turns through nothing.

mundane theorem three-rays
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let X be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume A ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  assume B ≠ Q                                                        (H6)
  assume X ≠ Q                                                        (H7)
  then        there is k ∈ ℤ with ∡AQX + ∡XQB = ∡AQB + 2·k·π
  metamath    gsang3, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from eflogeq
  target      gsang3
  note        Three rays from Q: turning from QA to QX and then on to QB
              turns from QA to QB, give or take whole turns, since each
              signed angle is kept between −π and π.

mundane theorem isosceles-signed-angles
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume A ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume B ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H6)
  assume |AQ| = |BQ|                                                  (H7)
  then        ∡QAB = ∡ABQ
  metamath    gisosctr, set.mm's isosctr, which this corpus states of its
              angle in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gisosctr with C := Q
  note        The base angles of an isosceles triangle, each turned the same
              way round it, are equal as signed angles: from AQ to AB at A
              and from BA to BQ at B. isosceles-angles says it unsigned.

mundane theorem same-side-turns-alike
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume C and D are on the same side of line AB                      (H5)
  then        ∡ACB·∡ADB > 0
  metamath    gsamesang, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gsideang
  target      gsamesang
  note        Two points on one side of a line see a segment of it turned
              the same way: the signed angle ACB is positive when C is on the
              counterclockwise side of AB and negative on the other.

mundane theorem triangle-angle-real
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        ∠QPR ∈ ℝ. ∠PQR ∈ ℝ. ∠QRP ∈ ℝ.
  metamath    gtriangp, gtriangq, gtriangr, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gangbnd3
  target      gtriangp, gtriangq, gtriangr
  note        One target to a sentence. mun:stdlib/geometry/angle-real asks
              that the corner differ from both ends, and the triangle says its
              points differ in one order each, so citing it at a corner takes a
              rotated triangle first; this says each angle is a number from the
              triangle as written.

mundane definition collinear
  open        Incidence is a primitive of the geometry, and the readable layer
              cannot state what it is. In the plane it says P = Q or
              (R − P)/(Q − P) is real, which is true in any order of the three
              points, so the notation's points stand in no order; and
              subtraction takes numbers while P, Q and R are points:
              the sorts that stop |CA| reading as a product stop this too. The
              notation carries a target, so a claim of collinearity elaborates;
              what has no readable statement is the equivalence.
  note        Named by the merge because mun:stdlib/geometry/triangle needs a word for what a
              triangle's vertices are not.

mundane definition triangle
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        P, Q, R form a triangle ↔ P ≠ Q and Q ≠ R and P ≠ R and
              not (P, Q, R are collinear)
  metamath    gtridef, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: the notation's target is the three
              inequalities and the quotient it denies, and collinearity is the
              first two one point or the quotient real, which with the points
              distinct is the quotient
  target      gtridef
  note        set.mm has no single notion. "Form a triangle" and "are
              collinear" are notations whose targets make the two sides one
              formula; what collinearity is in words is
              mun:stdlib/geometry/collinear, which this statement does not
              need. That the three are points is the three let lines, not a
              clause of the statement; the sorts carry it.

definition congruent
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let P′ be a point                                                   (H4)
  let Q′ be a point                                                   (H5)
  let R′ be a point                                                   (H6)
  then        triangle PQR ≅ triangle P′Q′R′ ↔ |PQ| = |P′Q′|, |QR| = |Q′R′|,
              |RP| = |R′P′|, ∠PQR = ∠P′Q′R′, ∠QRP = ∠Q′R′P′, and
              ∠RPQ = ∠R′P′Q′
  metamath    biid, since the notation's target is the six equations
  target      biid
  note        set.mm has no congruence of triangles, and the notation stands
              for the six equations themselves, so the two sides are one
              formula.

mundane definition isosceles
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        △PQR is isosceles ↔ P, Q, R form a triangle and
              (|PQ| = |PR| or |QP| = |QR| or |RP| = |RQ|)
  metamath    biid, since the notation's target is the triangle and the
              three equalities it chooses among
  target      biid
  note        Two sides equal, whichever two: a triangle named by its three
              points has no apex until a proof says which equality holds.

mundane definition equilateral
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        △PQR is equilateral ↔ P, Q, R form a triangle and |PQ| = |QR|
              and |QR| = |RP|
  metamath    biid, since the notation's target is the triangle and the two
              equalities
  target      biid

mundane definition right-angled
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let V be a point                                                    (H4)
  then        △PQR is right-angled at V ↔ P, Q, R form a triangle and
              ((V = P and ∠QPR = 90°) or (V = Q and ∠PQR = 90°) or
              (V = R and ∠PRQ = 90°))
  metamath    biid, since the notation's target is the triangle and the
              right angle at whichever vertex V is
  target      biid
  note        V is one of the three vertices and the angle there is right.

mundane theorem right-angled-at-vertex
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        △PQR is right-angled at R ↔ P, Q, R form a triangle and
              ∠PRQ = 90°
  metamath    grightat3
  target      grightat3
  note        Named at the vertex it writes last, a right angle is the angle
              there: the triangle's R ≠ P and R ≠ Q rule out the other two.

mundane theorem distance-symmetric
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  then        |PQ| = |QP|
  metamath    abssub
  target      abssub

mundane theorem angle-symmetric
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        ∠PQR = ∠RQP
  metamath    gangsym3, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm.
              The angle it writes is unsigned, which is what makes this true:
              ang itself is signed, and the two directions differ in sign. Off
              the branch cut that is arginv; on the cut nothing is negated and
              lognegb gives both directions as π. So the proof is in two cases
              and the absolute values agree either way.
  target      gangsym3

mundane theorem angle-turned
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        ∠PQR = ∠RQP
  metamath    gangsym3, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gangsym3
  note        As angle-symmetric, for points that may lie on a line: the
              angle between two arms does not depend on which is named first.

mundane definition parallel
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H5)
  assume C ≠ D                                                        (H6)
  then        line AB ∥ line CD ↔ line AB = line CD or not (there is
              X ∈ 𝔼² with X is on line AB and X is on line CD)
  metamath    gpardef, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: in the plane line AB ∥ line CD
              says (B − A)/(D − C) is real, which is true just where the two
              lines are one or share no point
  target      gpardef
  note        Two lines are parallel when they are one line or never meet,
              as SMSG defines it of distinct lines. Named for the angle sum,
              whose proof draws the parallel to one side through the
              opposite vertex.

mundane definition between
  open        In the plane R is between S and T says S ≠ R and
              (T − R)/(S − R) is a negative real: T lies beyond R from S, on
              the same line. The readable layer cannot state it, as for
              mun:stdlib/geometry/collinear.

mundane definition on-line
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let A be a point                                                    (H2)
  let B be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        P is on line AB ↔ A, B, P are collinear
  metamath    biid, since both notations' targets are the one quotient
  target      biid

mundane definition line
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let A be a point                                                    (H2)
  let B be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        P ∈ line AB ↔ P is on line AB
  metamath    glinel, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from elrab3: line AB is the set of
              the points P is on line AB says of P
  target      glinel
  note        The line through A and B as a set, for a statement that
              speaks of the line itself. A ≠ B where it is a line.

mundane definition ray
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let A be a point                                                    (H2)
  let B be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H4)
  then        P ∈ ray AB ↔ P = A or P = B or P is between A and B or
              B is between A and P
  metamath    grayel, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from elrab3: (P − A)/(B − A) is
              0, 1, between them or above 1
  target      grayel
  note        The ray from A through B: the segment AB and the points
              beyond B.

mundane theorem line-turned
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  then        line AB = line BA
  metamath    glineturn, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: the three points turned round
              are collinear still, by gcolrotate and gcolswap
  target      glineturn

mundane theorem on-line-cases
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume Q ≠ R                                                        (H4)
  assume P is on line QR                                              (H5)
  then        P = Q or P is between Q and R or P = R or R is between Q and P
              or Q is between P and R
  metamath    gonline, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: P is Q + t(R − Q) with t real,
              and t is 0, between 0 and 1, 1, above 1 or below 0
  target      gonline
  note        A point of a line through two points is one of them, between
              them, or beyond one of them, which a figure shows and a proof
              takes as the cases there are.

mundane definition perpendicular
  open        In the plane line AB ⊥ line CD says A ≠ B, C ≠ D and
              (D − C)/(B − A) has real part 0: the two directions are at a
              right angle. The readable layer cannot state it, as for
              mun:stdlib/geometry/parallel; where the lines meet,
              mun:stdlib/geometry/perpendicular-by-right-angle reads it as
              an angle.

mundane theorem perpendicular-by-right-angle
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        line QP ⊥ line QR ↔ ∠PQR = 90°
  metamath    gperp90, from angrteqvd
  target      gperp90
  note        Two lines through Q are perpendicular when the angle between
              them at Q is a right angle.

mundane definition midpoint
  let M be a point                                                    (H1)
  let A be a point                                                    (H2)
  let B be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        M is the midpoint of AB ↔ M is between A and B and |AM| = |MB|
  metamath    biid, since the notation's target is the betweenness and the
              equal halves
  target      biid
  note        In the plane the midpoint is (A + B)/2, which the page does not
              write: it adds points.

mundane theorem midpoint-unique
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let M be a point                                                    (H3)
  let N be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume M is the midpoint of AB                                      (H5)
  assume N is the midpoint of AB                                      (H6)
  then        M = N
  metamath    gmiduniq, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gmidval: each is (A + B)/2
  target      gmiduniq
  note        A segment has one midpoint, which is what "the midpoint of AB"
              takes for granted.

mundane theorem midpoint-determines-end
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let M be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume M is the midpoint of AB                                      (H5)
  assume M is the midpoint of AC                                      (H6)
  then        B = C
  metamath    gmidend, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gmidval: (A + B)/2 =
              (A + C)/2 cancels to B = C
  target      gmidend
  note        The reflection of a point through another is one point: B is
              the point beyond M from A at the distance |AM|.

mundane definition same-side
  open        Which side of the line through P and Q a point X is on is the
              sign of the imaginary part of (X − P)/(Q − P): positive on one
              side, negative on the other, zero on the line. Two points are on
              the same side when the two signs multiply to a positive number,
              and on opposite sides when to a negative one. The readable
              layer cannot state it, as for mun:stdlib/geometry/collinear.
  note        One record for both notations, same-side and opposite-sides,
              since they are one sign read two ways.

axiom supplementary-angles
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume B is between A and C                                         (H5)
  assume D ≠ B                                                        (H6)
  then        ∠ABD + ∠DBC = 180°
  metamath    gsupp, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from cosangneg2d, cospim and cos11
  target      gsupp
  note        Two angles on a line, Euclid I.13: with B on the segment AC,
              the angles ABD and DBC make a straight angle wherever D is.
              SMSG's postulate 14, the supplement postulate: two angles
              that form a linear pair are supplementary.

theorem triangle-inequality-points
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        |PR| ≤ |PQ| + |QR|
  metamath    abs3dif
  target      abs3dif

mundane theorem angle-addition
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let M be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume A, B, C form a triangle                                      (H5)
  assume M is between A and B                                         (H6)
  then        ∠ACM + ∠MCB = ∠ACB
  metamath    gangadd, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: set.mm adds no two angles
  target      gangadd
  note        A point of the side AB splits the angle at C in two. With T
              the point beyond C from A, angles-on-a-line makes ACM, MCB
              and BCT a straight angle, and supplementary-angles makes ACB
              and BCT one: M and B lie on one side of line CA, and A and B
              on two sides of line CM, which is what the line through M
              does.

mundane theorem distance-nonneg
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  then        |PQ| ≥ 0
  metamath    gdistnn, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from absge0
  target      gdistnn

mundane axiom distance-positive
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H3)
  then        |PQ| > 0
  metamath    gdistpos, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from absgt0
  target      gdistpos
  note        SMSG's postulate 2, the distance postulate: two distinct
              points are a positive distance apart.

mundane theorem distance-zero
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  then        |PQ| = 0 ↔ P = Q
  metamath    gdist0, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from abs00 and subeq0
  target      gdist0
  note        A point is at distance 0 from itself and from no other point.

mundane theorem distance-by-coordinates
  let a ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  let b ∈ ℝ                                                           (H2)
  let c ∈ ℝ                                                           (H3)
  let d ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  then        |(a, b)(c, d)| = √((a − c)² + (b − d)²)
  metamath    gdistco, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from absreim
  target      gdistco

mundane theorem collinear-by-coordinates
  let a ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  let b ∈ ℝ                                                           (H2)
  let c ∈ ℝ                                                           (H3)
  let d ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  let e ∈ ℝ                                                           (H5)
  let f ∈ ℝ                                                           (H6)
  then        (a, b), (c, d), (e, f) are collinear ↔ (c − a)·(f − b) = (e − a)·(d − b)
  metamath    gcolco, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from gdivre: a quotient is real just where the first times
              the conjugate of the second has imaginary part 0
  target      gcolco

mundane theorem between-by-coordinates
  let a ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  let b ∈ ℝ                                                           (H2)
  let c ∈ ℝ                                                           (H3)
  let d ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  let e ∈ ℝ                                                           (H5)
  let f ∈ ℝ                                                           (H6)
  then        (c, d) is between (a, b) and (e, f) ↔ (a − c)·(f − d) = (e − c)·(b − d) and (a − c)·(e − c) + (b − d)·(f − d) < 0
  metamath    gbetco, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from gdivneg: a quotient is a negative real just where the
              first times the conjugate of the second has imaginary part 0
              and negative real part
  target      gbetco
  note        The three points are collinear, and the two ends lie on
              opposite sides of the middle one: the differences from it
              point opposite ways, so their dot product is negative.

mundane theorem points-apart-by-coordinates
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let a ∈ ℝ                                                           (H3)
  let b ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  let c ∈ ℝ                                                           (H5)
  let d ∈ ℝ                                                           (H6)
  assume A = (a, b)                                                   (H7)
  assume B = (c, d)                                                   (H8)
  then        A ≠ B ↔ (a, b) ≠ (c, d)
  metamath    gptapart, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from cru and opthneg: each
              differs just where a coordinate does
  target      gptapart
  note        On the left (a, b) and (c, d) are points, as A and B are;
              on the right they are pairs, which `arithmetic` compares
              coordinate by coordinate (`METHODS.md`, Pairs).

mundane theorem distance-of-points-by-coordinates
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let a ∈ ℝ                                                           (H3)
  let b ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  let c ∈ ℝ                                                           (H5)
  let d ∈ ℝ                                                           (H6)
  assume A = (a, b)                                                   (H7)
  assume B = (c, d)                                                   (H8)
  then        |AB| = √((a − c)² + (b − d)²)
  metamath    gptdist, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gdistco, the points put for
              their coordinates
  target      gptdist
  note        distance-by-coordinates for two points named by letters, so a
              proof that gives its points coordinates reads each distance
              off in one step.

mundane theorem between-symmetric
  let R be a point                                                    (H1)
  let S be a point                                                    (H2)
  let T be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume R is between S and T                                         (H4)
  then        R is between T and S
  metamath    gbetsym, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm:
              the quotient read the other way is its reciprocal
  target      gbetsym

mundane theorem between-apart
  let R be a point                                                    (H1)
  let S be a point                                                    (H2)
  let T be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume R is between S and T                                         (H4)
  then        S ≠ R. T ≠ R.
  metamath    gbetne0, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: the first is part of what
              between says, and T = R would make the quotient 0, which
              is not negative
  target      gbetne0
  note        A point between two others is apart from both, so the
              angles and triangles at it are defined.

mundane theorem between-ends-apart
  let R be a point                                                    (H1)
  let S be a point                                                    (H2)
  let T be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume R is between S and T                                         (H4)
  then        S ≠ T
  metamath    gbetends, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: S = T would make the quotient
              1, which is not negative
  target      gbetends
  note        A segment with a point strictly between its ends has two ends.

mundane theorem between-collinear
  let R be a point                                                    (H1)
  let S be a point                                                    (H2)
  let T be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume R is between S and T                                         (H4)
  then        T, R, S are collinear
  metamath    gbetcol, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gtricol
  target      gbetcol
  note        A point between two others is on their line.

mundane theorem triangle-on-side
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let S be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H5)
  assume S is between Q and R                                         (H6)
  then        P, Q, S form a triangle
  metamath    gtriside, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: set.mm has no triangles over ℂ
  target      gtriside
  note        A point of the side QR, apart from its ends, makes a smaller
              triangle with the vertex opposite and either end: S lies on
              line QR, which P does not.

mundane theorem angle-along-segment
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let S be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume S is between P and Q                                         (H5)
  assume R ≠ P                                                        (H6)
  then        ∠SPR = ∠QPR
  metamath    gangseg, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gangseg
  note        S on the segment from P to Q is on the ray from P through Q,
              so the angle that ray makes with PR is the same measured to S
              as to Q.

mundane theorem segment-addition
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let S be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume S is between P and Q                                         (H4)
  then        |PS| + |SQ| = |PQ|
  metamath    gsegadd, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gsegadd

mundane theorem sine-positive
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        sin(∠PQR) > 0
  metamath    gsinpos, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gsinpos
  note        An angle of a triangle is strictly between 0° and 180°, where
              the sine is positive.

theorem law-of-sines
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        |PQ|·sin(∠PQR) = |PR|·sin(∠QRP)
  metamath    glawsin, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from heron, taken at Q and at R
  target      glawsin
  note        Each side over the sine of the angle opposite it is the same:
              PR is opposite Q and PQ opposite R, so |PR|/sin(∠PQR) =
              |PQ|/sin(∠QRP), written without division.

mundane definition area
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        the area of △PQR = (1/2)·(|QR|·|PR|)·|sin(∡QRP)|
  metamath    eqid, since the notation's target is the right side
  target      eqid
  note        Half of two sides times the sine of the angle between them,
              the sine's size taken so that the area does not depend on which
              way the triangle runs.

theorem area-by-sine
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ R                                                        (H4)
  assume Q ≠ R                                                        (H5)
  then        the area of △PQR = (1/2)·(|QR|·|PR|)·sin(∠QRP)
  metamath    garea, from gsinabs
  target      garea

mundane theorem area-real
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ R                                                        (H4)
  assume Q ≠ R                                                        (H5)
  then        the area of △PQR ∈ ℝ
  metamath    gareacl, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gsangcl
  target      gareacl

mundane axiom area-positive
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        the area of △PQR > 0
  metamath    gareapos, from gsinpos
  target      gareapos
  note        SMSG's postulate 17, said of a triangle: a triangle has a
              positive area.

theorem heron
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let s ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  assume P ≠ R                                                        (H5)
  assume Q ≠ R                                                        (H6)
  assume s = (|QR| + |PR| + |PQ|)/2                                   (H7)
  then        the area of △PQR = √(s·(s − |QR|)·((s − |PR|)·(s − |PQ|)))
  metamath    gheron, from heron
  target      gheron
  note        Heron's formula: the area from the three sides alone, s being
              half the perimeter.

theorem law-of-cosines
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        |RP|² = |PQ|² + |QR|² − 2·(|PQ|·|QR|·cos(∠PQR))
  metamath    glawcos, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from lawcos
  target      glawcos
  note        The side opposite Q, from the two sides at Q and the angle
              between them; at a right angle the last term is 0 and it is
              Pythagoras.

mundane theorem angle-equal-by-cosine
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let P′ be a point                                                   (H4)
  let Q′ be a point                                                   (H5)
  let R′ be a point                                                   (H6)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H7)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H8)
  assume P′ ≠ Q′                                                      (H9)
  assume R′ ≠ Q′                                                      (H10)
  assume cos(∠PQR) = cos(∠P′Q′R′)                                     (H11)
  then        ∠PQR = ∠P′Q′R′
  metamath    gangeq3, from cos11
  target      gangeq3
  note        An angle is between 0° and 180°, where the cosine is one-to-one.

mundane theorem angle-order-by-cosine
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let P′ be a point                                                   (H4)
  let Q′ be a point                                                   (H5)
  let R′ be a point                                                   (H6)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H7)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H8)
  assume P′ ≠ Q′                                                      (H9)
  assume R′ ≠ Q′                                                      (H10)
  then        ∠PQR < ∠P′Q′R′ ↔ cos(∠P′Q′R′) < cos(∠PQR)
  metamath    gangord3, from cosord
  target      gangord3
  note        An angle is between 0° and 180°, where the cosine falls: the
              larger angle has the smaller cosine.

mundane theorem right-angle-by-cosine
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        ∠PQR = 90° ↔ cos(∠PQR) = 0
  metamath    gright3, from coseq00topi
  target      gright3

mundane theorem right-angle-triangle
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  assume ∠PQR = 90°                                                   (H6)
  then        P, Q, R form a triangle
  metamath    grttri, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gperp90: the quotient of the
              two arms has real part 0, so it is real only where it is 0
  target      grttri
  note        The arms of a right angle are not on one line.

mundane theorem cosine-right-triangle
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ R                                                        (H4)
  assume Q ≠ R                                                        (H5)
  assume ∠PRQ = 90°                                                   (H6)
  then        |QR| = |QP|·cos(∠RQP)
  metamath    gcosrt, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from glawcos, at Q and at the
              right angle
  target      gcosrt
  note        In a triangle with a right angle at R, the side QR next to
              the angle at Q is the hypotenuse times that angle's cosine:
              the cosine is the adjacent side over the hypotenuse, which is
              how a school reader meets it.

mundane theorem angle-at-most-straight
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        ∠PQR ≤ 180°
  metamath    gangle180, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gangicc3
  target      gangle180

mundane theorem angle-by-cosine
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let x ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H6)
  assume 0 ≤ x                                                        (H7)
  assume x ≤ 180°                                                     (H8)
  then        ∠PQR = x ↔ cos(∠PQR) = cos(x)
  metamath    gangcos, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from gangicc3 and cos11
  target      gangcos
  note        An angle is read from its cosine, as right-angle-by-cosine
              reads a right angle: on [0°, 180°] the cosine takes each value
              once.

axiom side-angle-side
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let P′ be a point                                                   (H4)
  let Q′ be a point                                                   (H5)
  let R′ be a point                                                   (H6)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H7)
  assume P′, Q′, R′ form a triangle                                   (H8)
  assume |PQ| = |P′Q′|                                                (H9)
  assume ∠PQR = ∠P′Q′R′                                               (H10)
  assume |QR| = |Q′R′|                                                (H11)
  then        triangle PQR ≅ triangle P′Q′R′
  metamath    gsas, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm. An
              axiom in Euclid and in Hilbert, because a synthetic geometry has
              no coordinates to compute with and congruence has to be
              stipulated; over ℂ it is a theorem. The proof is the law of
              cosines at each of the three vertices, six applications in all,
              which are three cyclic rotations of glawcos in each triangle.
              The third side comes first, since equal sides and equal cosines
              make equal squares and a length is not negative; with all three
              sides equal the other two angles follow the other way round.
  target      gsas
  note        The isosceles proof instantiates A, B, C, A′, B′, C′, which are not
              this statement's variable names. One of the two must change; the
              merge changed neither. See DATABASE.md.

# The postulates of plane geometry: the School Mathematics Study Group's,
# those of the plane, with the area postulates said of triangles. A reader
# takes each as given, and each is proved over ℂ in
# elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm, so that nothing is assumed. Four are
# records above: distance-positive (2), supplementary-angles (14),
# side-angle-side (15) and area-positive (17).

axiom line-uniqueness
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H5)
  assume C ≠ D                                                        (H6)
  assume A is on line CD                                              (H7)
  assume B is on line CD                                              (H8)
  then        line AB = line CD
  metamath    glineuniq, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: a point's place along line AB
              is a real affine change of its place along line CD
  target      glineuniq
  note        SMSG's postulate 1: two distinct points lie on exactly one
              line. Lines are said by two of their points, so the postulate
              says that any two distinct points of a line make that line.

axiom three-points
  then        there are P, Q, R ∈ 𝔼² with P, Q, R form a triangle
  metamath    gthreepts, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: 0, 1 and i, since i is not real
  target      gthreepts
  note        SMSG's postulate 5, of the plane: it holds three points not on
              one line.

axiom angle-measure
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        ∠PQR > 0°. ∠PQR < 180°.
  metamath    gangmeas, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: the angle is in [0, π] and its
              sine is positive, and the sine is 0 at both ends
  target      gangmeas
  note        SMSG's postulate 11: an angle, two rays from one point not on
              one line, has a measure strictly between 0° and 180°.
              mun:stdlib/geometry/angle-real and
              mun:stdlib/geometry/angle-at-most-straight say what holds of
              any three points, the ends apart from the corner.

axiom ruler
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H3)
  then        there is f : line AB → ℝ with f : line AB → ℝ is one-to-one and
              onto and for all P, Q ∈ line AB, |PQ| = |f(P) − f(Q)|
  metamath    gruler, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: f takes P to (P − A)/(B − A)
              times |AB|, which is real on the line
  target      gruler
  note        SMSG's postulate 3, the ruler: the points of a line are paired
              with the real numbers so that a distance is a difference of
              coordinates.

axiom ruler-placement
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H3)
  then        there is f : line AB → ℝ with f : line AB → ℝ is one-to-one and
              onto and f(A) = 0 and f(B) > 0 and for all P, Q ∈ line AB,
              |PQ| = |f(P) − f(Q)|
  metamath    grulerplace, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm, with the f of gruler
  target      grulerplace
  note        SMSG's postulate 4: a ruler may be laid along a line with any
              point of it at 0 and any other on the positive side.

axiom parallel-uniqueness
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let C be a point                                                    (H4)
  let D be a point                                                    (H5)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H6)
  assume not (P is on line AB)                                        (H7)
  assume P ≠ C                                                        (H8)
  assume P ≠ D                                                        (H9)
  assume line PC ∥ line AB                                            (H10)
  assume line PD ∥ line AB                                            (H11)
  then        line PC = line PD
  metamath    gparuniq, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: both lines run the way B − A
              does, so D − P is a real multiple of C − P
  target      gparuniq
  note        SMSG's postulate 16, the parallel postulate: through a point
              not on a line there is at most one line parallel to it. That
              there is one is thm:stdlib/geometry/parallel-through.

axiom plane-separation
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H5)
  assume not (P is on line AB)                                        (H6)
  assume not (Q is on line AB)                                        (H7)
  then        P and Q are on the same side of line AB or P and Q are on
              opposite sides of line AB
  metamath    gplanesep, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: neither point's side of the
              line is 0, so their product is positive or negative
  target      gplanesep
  note        SMSG's postulate 9, the plane separation postulate, in three
              parts: a line divides the points off it into two sides, each
              side holds a segment between two of its points
              (plane-separation-convex), and a segment between the two
              sides meets the line (plane-separation-crossing).

axiom plane-separation-convex
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  let X be a point                                                    (H5)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H6)
  assume P and Q are on the same side of line AB                      (H7)
  assume X is between P and Q                                         (H8)
  then        X and P are on the same side of line AB
  metamath    gplanesepcvx, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: X's side is a weighted mean of
              P's and Q's
  target      gplanesepcvx
  note        SMSG's postulate 9, second part: each side of a line is
              convex.

axiom plane-separation-crossing
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H5)
  assume P and Q are on opposite sides of line AB                     (H6)
  then        there is X ∈ 𝔼² with X is on line AB and X is between P and Q
  metamath    gplanesepcross, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: the point of PQ whose side is 0
  target      gplanesepcross
  note        SMSG's postulate 9, third part: a segment joining the two
              sides of a line meets the line.

axiom angle-construction
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let x ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  assume A, B, C form a triangle                                      (H5)
  assume x > 0°                                                       (H6)
  assume x < 180°                                                     (H7)
  then        there is D ∈ 𝔼² with D and C are on the same side of line AB
              and ∠BAD = x
  metamath    gangconsm, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gangcons: D is A plus B − A
              turned through x toward C's side
  target      gangconsm
  note        SMSG's postulate 12, the angle construction postulate: on a
              given side of a line, a ray from a point of it makes each
              angle from 0° to 180° with the line, in two parts. This is
              that there is one; angle-construction-unique that there is
              only one.

axiom angle-construction-unique
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  let E be a point                                                    (H5)
  assume A, B, C form a triangle                                      (H6)
  assume D and C are on the same side of line AB                      (H7)
  assume E and C are on the same side of line AB                      (H8)
  assume ∠BAD = ∠BAE                                                  (H9)
  then        E ∈ ray AD
  metamath    gangconsuniq, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: on one side of the line, the
              angle fixes the argument of (D − A)/(B − A)
  target      gangconsuniq
  note        SMSG's postulate 12, second part: the ray is the only one.

axiom angle-addition-inside
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume A, B, C form a triangle                                      (H5)
  assume D and C are on the same side of line AB                      (H6)
  assume D and B are on the same side of line AC                      (H7)
  then        ∠BAD + ∠DAC = ∠BAC
  metamath    gangaddin, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: the arguments of the two
              quotients add, and both lie on one side of the real line
  target      gangaddin
  note        SMSG's postulate 13, the angle addition postulate: D is
              inside angle BAC, on C's side of line AB and B's side of line
              AC. mun:stdlib/geometry/angle-addition says the same of a D
              between B and C.

axiom area-congruent
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let P′ be a point                                                   (H4)
  let Q′ be a point                                                   (H5)
  let R′ be a point                                                   (H6)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H7)
  assume P′, Q′, R′ form a triangle                                   (H8)
  assume triangle PQR ≅ triangle P′Q′R′                               (H9)
  then        the area of △PQR = the area of △P′Q′R′
  metamath    gareacong, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from garea: two sides and the
              angle between them fix the area
  target      gareacong
  note        SMSG's postulate 18: congruent triangles have the same area.

axiom area-base-height
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let H be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H5)
  assume H is on line QR                                              (H6)
  assume line PH ⊥ line QR                                            (H7)
  then        the area of △PQR = (1/2)·|QR|·|PH|
  metamath    gareabh, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: |PH| is |PR| times the sine of
              the angle at R
  target      gareabh
  note        SMSG's postulate 20, said of a triangle: half the base times
              the height, where SMSG says it of a rectangle.

axiom area-addition
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H5)
  assume D is between Q and R                                         (H6)
  then        the area of △PQR = the area of △PQD + the area of △PDR
  metamath    gareaadd, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: the three triangles have one
              height over line QR, and QD and DR make QR
  target      gareaadd
  note        SMSG's postulate 19, said of a triangle cut in two by a
              segment from a vertex.

# Facts of order and of the sides of a line that a proof from the
# postulates takes for granted, as a school text does: each is proved over
# ℂ in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm.

mundane theorem midpoint-exists
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H3)
  then        there is M ∈ 𝔼² with M is the midpoint of AB
  metamath    gmidex, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: (A + B)/2
  target      gmidex

mundane theorem segment-extension
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let r ∈ ℝ                                                           (H3)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H4)
  assume r > 0                                                        (H5)
  then        there is X ∈ 𝔼² with B is between A and X and |BX| = r
  metamath    gextend, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: B plus r times the unit
              direction from A to B
  target      gextend
  note        Euclid's second postulate, a segment produced, with the length
              it is produced by.

mundane theorem segment-point
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let r ∈ ℝ                                                           (H3)
  assume r > 0                                                        (H4)
  assume r < |AB|                                                     (H5)
  then        there is X ∈ 𝔼² with X is between A and B and |AX| = r
  metamath    gsegpt, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: A plus r times the unit
              direction from A to B
  target      gsegpt
  note        A length shorter than a segment, laid off along it from one
              end.

mundane theorem triangle-off-line
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume Q ≠ R                                                        (H4)
  assume not (P is on line QR)                                        (H5)
  then        P, Q, R form a triangle
  metamath    gtrioff, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gtrioff

mundane theorem triangle-turned
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        P, R, Q form a triangle
  metamath    gtriturn, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from goffswapd
  target      gtriturn
  note        The same triangle with its last two vertices named the other
              way round.

mundane theorem triangle-rotated
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        Q, R, P form a triangle
  metamath    gtrirot, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gtrinotd
  target      gtrirot
  note        The same triangle named from its second vertex. With
              triangle-turned it names a triangle in any order.

mundane theorem ray-on-line
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let A be a point                                                    (H2)
  let B be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ∈ ray AB                                                   (H4)
  then        P is on line AB
  metamath    grayline, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      grayline
  note        A ray lies on its line.

mundane theorem same-not-opposite
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume P and Q are on the same side of line AB                      (H5)
  then        not (P and Q are on opposite sides of line AB)
  metamath    gsidenot, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gsidenot
  note        The two sides of a line do not meet, which SMSG's plane
              separation postulate says in calling them two sets.

mundane theorem parallel-turned
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H5)
  assume line AB ∥ line CD                                            (H6)
  then        line CD ∥ line AB
  metamath    gparturn, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gparturn
  note        Being parallel goes both ways.

mundane theorem perpendicular-turned
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume line AB ⊥ line CD                                            (H5)
  then        line BA ⊥ line CD
  metamath    gperpturn, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gperpturn
  note        Line BA is line AB, so a line perpendicular to the one is
              perpendicular to the other.

mundane theorem perpendicular-along
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  let E be a point                                                    (H5)
  let F be a point                                                    (H6)
  assume line AB ⊥ line CD                                            (H7)
  assume E is on line CD                                              (H8)
  assume F is on line CD                                              (H9)
  assume E ≠ F                                                        (H10)
  then        line AB ⊥ line EF
  metamath    gperpalong, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gperpalong
  note        Being perpendicular belongs to the line, not to the two
              points that name it.

mundane theorem area-rotated
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        the area of △PQR = the area of △QRP
  metamath    garearot, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: each is half the size of the
              imaginary part of (P − R) times the conjugate of (Q − R)
  target      garearot
  note        The area of a triangle does not depend on which vertex is
              named first.

mundane theorem same-side-turned
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume P and Q are on the same side of line AB                      (H5)
  then        Q and P are on the same side of line AB
  metamath    gsameturn, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gsameturn

mundane theorem opposite-sides-turned
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume P and Q are on opposite sides of line AB                     (H5)
  then        Q and P are on opposite sides of line AB
  metamath    goppturn, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      goppturn

mundane theorem same-side-same
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  let R be a point                                                    (H5)
  assume P and Q are on the same side of line AB                      (H6)
  assume Q and R are on the same side of line AB                      (H7)
  then        P and R are on the same side of line AB
  metamath    gsametr, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gsametr

mundane theorem opposite-opposite-same
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  let R be a point                                                    (H5)
  assume P and Q are on opposite sides of line AB                     (H6)
  assume Q and R are on opposite sides of line AB                     (H7)
  then        P and R are on the same side of line AB
  metamath    goppopp, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      goppopp

mundane theorem same-opposite-opposite
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  let R be a point                                                    (H5)
  assume P and Q are on the same side of line AB                      (H6)
  assume Q and R are on opposite sides of line AB                     (H7)
  then        P and R are on opposite sides of line AB
  metamath    gsameopp, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gsameopp

mundane theorem same-side-of-line
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  let P be a point                                                    (H5)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H6)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H7)
  assume C ≠ D                                                        (H8)
  assume C is on line AB                                              (H9)
  assume D is on line AB                                              (H10)
  assume P and Q are on the same side of line AB                      (H11)
  then        P and Q are on the same side of line CD
  metamath    gsideline, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: a point's place along line CD
              is a real affine change of its place along line AB
  target      gsideline
  note        Which side a point is on belongs to the line, not to the two
              points that name it.

mundane theorem opposite-sides-of-line
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  let P be a point                                                    (H5)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H6)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H7)
  assume C ≠ D                                                        (H8)
  assume C is on line AB                                              (H9)
  assume D is on line AB                                              (H10)
  assume P and Q are on opposite sides of line AB                     (H11)
  then        P and Q are on opposite sides of line CD
  metamath    goppline, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm, as gsideline
  target      goppline

mundane theorem segment-off-line
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  let X be a point                                                    (H5)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H6)
  assume Q is on line AB                                              (H7)
  assume not (P is on line AB)                                        (H8)
  assume X is between P and Q                                         (H9)
  then        X and P are on the same side of line AB
  metamath    gsegside, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gsegside
  note        A segment from a point off a line to a point on it stays on
              the first point's side.

mundane theorem segment-across-line
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  let M be a point                                                    (H5)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H6)
  assume M is on line AB                                              (H7)
  assume not (P is on line AB)                                        (H8)
  assume M is between P and Q                                         (H9)
  then        P and Q are on opposite sides of line AB
  metamath    gsegcross, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gsegcross
  note        A segment through a point of a line, from a point off it,
              ends on the other side.

mundane theorem segment-beyond-off-line
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  let X be a point                                                    (H5)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H6)
  assume Q is on line AB                                              (H7)
  assume not (X is on line AB)                                        (H8)
  assume X is between P and Q                                         (H9)
  then        P and X are on the same side of line AB
  metamath    gsegbeyond, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gsegbeyond
  note        A segment from a point of a line through a point off it goes
              on, past that point, on its side.

mundane theorem same-side-off-line
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume P and Q are on the same side of line AB                      (H5)
  then        A ≠ B. not (P is on line AB). not (Q is on line AB).
  metamath    gsameoffab, gsameoffp, gsameoffq, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gsameoff
  target      gsameoffab, gsameoffp, gsameoffq
  note        One target to a sentence.

mundane theorem opposite-sides-off-line
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume P and Q are on opposite sides of line AB                     (H5)
  then        A ≠ B. not (P is on line AB). not (Q is on line AB).
  metamath    goppoffab, goppoffp, goppoffq, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from goppoff
  target      goppoffab, goppoffp, goppoffq
  note        One target to a sentence.

mundane theorem on-line-ends
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  then        A is on line AB. B is on line AB.
  metamath    gonends1, gonends2, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gonends
  target      gonends1, gonends2
  note        One target to a sentence.

mundane theorem triangle-not-on-line
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        not (P is on line QR)
  metamath    gtrinot, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gtrinot

mundane theorem triangle-extension
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let S be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H5)
  assume Q is between R and S                                         (H6)
  then        P, Q, S form a triangle
  metamath    gtriext, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gtriext
  note        A side produced past a vertex, as triangle-on-side takes a
              point within it.

mundane theorem off-line-apart
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let P be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume not (P is on line AB)                                        (H4)
  then        P ≠ A. P ≠ B.
  metamath    goffapart1, goffapart2, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from goffapart
  target      goffapart1, goffapart2
  note        One target to a sentence.

mundane theorem on-off-apart
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let X be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Y be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume X is on line AB                                              (H5)
  assume not (Y is on line AB)                                        (H6)
  then        X ≠ Y
  metamath    gonoffapart, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gonoffapart

mundane theorem off-line-through
  let R be a point                                                    (H1)
  let P be a point                                                    (H2)
  let S be a point                                                    (H3)
  let X be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume not (S is on line RP)                                        (H5)
  assume X is on line RS                                              (H6)
  assume X ≠ R                                                        (H7)
  then        not (X is on line RP)
  metamath    gofflinethru, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gofflinethru
  note        Two lines through R meet only at R.

mundane theorem line-crossing-between
  let R be a point                                                    (H1)
  let P be a point                                                    (H2)
  let S be a point                                                    (H3)
  let X be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume X is on line RS                                              (H5)
  assume S and X are on opposite sides of line RP                     (H6)
  then        R is between S and X
  metamath    glinecrossbet, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      glinecrossbet
  note        A line through R crosses another line through R at R, so a
              point of it on the far side is beyond R.

mundane theorem angle-same-ray
  let R be a point                                                    (H1)
  let P be a point                                                    (H2)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H3)
  let X be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume X is on line PQ                                              (H5)
  assume X and Q are on the same side of line PR                      (H6)
  then        ∠RPX = ∠RPQ
  metamath    gangsameray, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gangrp
  target      gangsameray
  note        A point of line PQ on Q's side of line PR is on the ray from
              P through Q, so it makes the same angle.

mundane theorem ray-side-transfer
  let R be a point                                                    (H1)
  let P be a point                                                    (H2)
  let S be a point                                                    (H3)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H4)
  let X be a point                                                    (H5)
  assume X is on line RP                                              (H6)
  assume X and P are on the same side of line RS                      (H7)
  assume not (P is on line RQ)                                        (H8)
  then        X and P are on the same side of line RQ
  metamath    graysidetr, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      graysidetr
  note        A point on the ray from R through P is on P's side of every
              line through R that P is off.

mundane theorem parallel-along
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  let E be a point                                                    (H5)
  assume line AB ∥ line CD                                            (H6)
  assume A ≠ B                                                        (H7)
  assume E is on line AB                                              (H8)
  assume E ≠ A                                                        (H9)
  then        line AE ∥ line DC
  metamath    gparalong, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gparalong
  note        Being parallel belongs to the two lines, not to the points
              that name them.

mundane theorem off-line-swap
  let R be a point                                                    (H1)
  let P be a point                                                    (H2)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume not (Q is on line RP)                                        (H4)
  then        not (P is on line RQ)
  metamath    goffswap, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      goffswap
